//! lotus/halve.rye — ALES85: the half-wave rectifier — keep the positive half, zero the negative, y = max(x, 0). The full-wave rectifier (ALES84)
//! folds the whole negative half of the wave UP onto the positive side (y = |x|, pure EVEN harmonics). The half-wave rectifier is its plain sibling: it
//! folds the negative half AWAY to silence instead of up. Where a diode BRIDGE (four diodes) makes full-wave rectification, a SINGLE diode makes
//! half-wave — it passes current one way and blocks it the other. The half-wave output is the single-diode clipper of a crystal radio and the octave-up
//! voice of a one-diode fuzz: the positive half of the wave kept whole, the negative half silenced.
//!
//! The crux is the complement of ALES84's: where |x| is exact for every i16 EXCEPT sample_min (whose magnitude 32768 overflows the rail and must
//! saturate), max(x, 0) is EXACT for EVERY i16 with NO rail edge at all — a non-negative sample passes unchanged (already within [0, sample_max]) and a
//! negative sample becomes 0; neither branch can overflow. The half-wave rectifier needs NO saturate. That absence is the whole distinction between the
//! two rectifiers: the bridge has one over-rail sample to pin; the single diode has none.
//!
//! Harmonically, stated honestly: the full-wave rectifier is a PURE EVEN generator (|x| is an even function); the half-wave rectifier is the average of
//! the dry wave and the full-wave rectified wave — max(x, 0) = (x + |x|) / 2 — so its output carries the FUNDAMENTAL (odd) plus the EVEN harmonics plus
//! a DC term: an EVEN+ODD MIX, not pure even. Half the amplitude of the full-wave move, half the DC. The map is MEMORYLESS — every output depends only
//! on its own input, so the in-place read-then-write touches nothing another step needs, and halving a span block by block equals halving it whole. It
//! is IDEMPOTENT — a non-negative sample maps to itself, so halve(halve(x)) = halve(x). And its output is always NON-NEGATIVE — the one-sided signal is
//! the rectifier's whole point, exactly as with full-wave.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES85.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260814-214355_fill-ales85-lotus-half-wave-rectifier.md
//! Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, sample_min/max. It carries NO gain, NO ceiling, and NO threshold — the threshold is fixed at zero,
//! and max(x, 0) has no parameter that could be illegal; a keeper who wants a boosted or negatively-clamped half-wave composes this with ALES78's drive
//! over the same span, both in-place span maps that compose.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM buffers on one bench, siloed to lotus/. No socket, no network, no keys, no
//! funds, no real device, no real sample rate — the shape is a half-wave threshold at zero, instantaneous (no attack/release, no anti-aliasing — a
//! rectifier doubles frequency and is a heavy harmonic generator, and its harmonics fold in the i16 domain exactly as any diode rectifier's). One
//! compare-and-keep per sample; no saturate is needed, since max(x, 0) never leaves the rail.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/halve.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");

pub const halve_version = "20260814.214355";

/// The half-wave rectifier's faults, one consistent name each. BadRange coincides by name with the full-wave rectifier, the sibling drives, and ALES2's
/// timeline (Zig merges error sets by name), so a composer sees one consistent span fault. The half-wave rectifier names NO other fault — it takes no
/// gain, no ceiling, no threshold: max(x, 0) has no parameter that could be illegal.
pub const HalveError = error{
    /// A span outside the current samples — refused, no write.
    BadRange,
};

/// Half-wave-rectify `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `clip`: each sample becomes `max(x, 0)`, the positive half of the wave kept whole
/// and the negative half folded away to silence. Exact for EVERY i16 — a non-negative sample passes unchanged, a negative sample becomes 0; there is no
/// rail edge and no saturate (the very fact that separates the single diode from the full-wave bridge). After the call every output is non-negative, at
/// most the input, and depends only on its own input (the rectifier is memoryless). Refuses BadRange on a span outside the samples — BEFORE any write,
/// so an illegal call leaves the clip exactly as it was. Values only — never the length.
pub fn halve(clip: *timeline.Clip, start: u32, count: u32) HalveError!void {
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples — checked without underflow (count vs the remainder), exactly as the full-wave sibling.
    if (start > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - start) return error.BadRange;

    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < count) : (k += 1) {
        const i: u32 = start + k;
        const dry: i16 = clip.buf[i];
        // invariant: the sample read is a legal i16 — it is audio, never a wide intermediate.
        assert(dry >= timeline.sample_min and dry <= timeline.sample_max);
        // The half-wave map: keep the positive half, silence the negative. No wide intermediate and no saturate are needed — a non-negative sample is
        // already within the rail, and a negative sample becomes 0, so max(x, 0) never leaves [0, sample_max]. This is the whole distinction from the
        // full-wave rectifier, whose lone most-negative sample had to saturate.
        const out: i16 = if (dry > 0) dry else 0;
        // invariant: the output is non-negative — a rectifier makes a one-sided signal, so the negative half is silenced, never passed.
        assert(out >= 0);
        // invariant: the output is exactly the half-wave map — a positive sample passes unchanged, a non-positive one drops to 0 — so the map only holds
        // or lowers, never raises, and touches only the sign of the wave.
        assert(if (dry > 0) out == dry else out == 0);
        clip.buf[i] = out;
    }
    // invariant: a halve changes sample values only, never the clip's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= clip.len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: NON-NEGATIVES ARE UNCHANGED (max(x,0) = x for x ≥ 0); NEGATIVES FOLD to SILENCE by hand (−x → 0, not up); NO RAIL EDGE (sample_min → 0,
//     never a saturate — the very fact that separates half-wave from full-wave); every output is NON-NEGATIVE and the map is IDEMPOTENT; the half-wave
//     output is the full-wave output MASKED to the positive half (a symmetric pair +a,−a → a,0 where full-wave gave a,a); the SPAN discipline holds; and
//     a span past the end refuses BadRange, the clip untouched ---

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. NON-NEGATIVES ARE UNCHANGED — max(x, 0) = x for every x ≥ 0, so the positive half of the wave passes through whole. A signal already one-sided
    //    half-wave-rectifies to itself, the way ALES8's unity fader reproduces the plain render.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 0, 1, 12345, 32767, 200, 5 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve(&clip, 0, clip.len);
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.NonNegChanged;
    }

    // 2. NEGATIVES FOLD TO SILENCE BY HAND — each negative sample drops to 0, the negative half of the wave folded AWAY (not up, as the full-wave
    //    rectifier does). −1→0, −100→0, −12345→0, −32767→0; the positives among them pass unchanged.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ -1, 100, -100, -12345, 32767, -32767 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve(&clip, 0, clip.len);
        const want = [_]i16{ 0, 100, 0, 0, 32767, 0 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.FoldWrong;
    }

    // 3. NO RAIL EDGE — where the full-wave rectifier had to SATURATE sample_min (magnitude 32768 overflows the rail), the half-wave rectifier maps
    //    sample_min straight to 0: max(sample_min, 0) = 0. There is no over-rail value to pin, so max(x, 0) is EXACT for every i16 — the very fact that
    //    separates the single diode from the full-wave bridge.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ timeline.sample_min, -32767, 0, 32767 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve(&clip, 0, clip.len);
        const want = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 32767 }; // sample_min → 0 (silenced, not saturated); 32767 → 32767 (exact)
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.RailEdgeWrong;
    }

    // 4. EVERY OUTPUT IS NON-NEGATIVE, AND THE MAP IS IDEMPOTENT — a rectifier makes a one-sided signal, so no output is below zero; and half-wave
    //    rectifying the already-rectified clip a second time changes nothing (a non-negative sample maps to itself).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, -200, 5000, -5000, timeline.sample_min, 0, -1, 32767 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve(&clip, 0, clip.len);
        const once = clip.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < once.len) : (i += 1) if (once[i] < 0) return error.NegativeOutput;
        var again = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&again, once);
        try halve(&again, 0, again.len);
        if (!eq(again.samples(), once)) return error.NotIdempotent;
    }

    // 5. THE HALF-WAVE OUTPUT IS THE FULL-WAVE OUTPUT MASKED TO THE POSITIVE HALF — for a symmetric pair +a and −a, half-wave keeps +a→a and −a→0,
    //    where the full-wave rectifier (ALES84) sent BOTH to a. Half-wave keeps the fundamental (the +a survives) and silences the mirror image; that is
    //    the even+odd mix, half the amplitude of the full-wave's pure-even doubling.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 6000, -6000, 300, -300 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve(&clip, 0, clip.len);
        const out = clip.samples();
        const want = [_]i16{ 6000, 0, 300, 0 }; // +a passes, −a silenced — full-wave would have given { 6000, 6000, 300, 300 }
        if (!eq(out, &want)) return error.NotMasked;
    }

    // 6. THE SPAN DISCIPLINE — half-wave-rectifying a sub-span leaves samples outside [start, count) exactly as they were, the in-place discipline the
    //    whole suite keeps. Halve the middle two of six; the first and last two are untouched, negatives among them preserved.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ -100, -200, -500, 500, -300, -400 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve(&clip, 2, 2); // indices 2,3 only: −500→0, 500→500
        const want = [_]i16{ -100, -200, 0, 500, -300, -400 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.SpanLeaked;
    }

    // 7. A SPAN past the end refuses BadRange — before any write, the clip untouched. The half-wave rectifier names no other fault (max(x, 0) has no
    //    parameter that could be illegal).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 9, -10, 11 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        if (halve(&clip, 1, 3)) |_| return error.OverRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongOverRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedOverRangeTouched;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-halve: the HALF-WAVE RECTIFIER — keep the positive half, zero the negative, y = max(x, 0). The full-wave rectifier (ALES84) " ++
        "folds the whole negative half UP onto the positive side (y = |x|, pure EVEN harmonics); the half-wave rectifier is its plain sibling — it " ++
        "folds the negative half AWAY to silence instead. Where a diode BRIDGE makes full-wave rectification, a SINGLE diode makes half-wave: it " ++
        "passes current one way and blocks it the other (the crystal-radio clipper, the one-diode fuzz). The crux is the complement of ALES84's: where " ++
        "|x| is exact for every i16 EXCEPT sample_min (which must saturate), max(x, 0) is EXACT for EVERY i16 with NO rail edge at all — a non-negative " ++
        "sample passes unchanged, a negative sample becomes 0, neither branch overflows, and NO saturate is needed. Harmonically it is the even+odd " ++
        "mix: max(x, 0) = (x + |x|) / 2 carries the fundamental (odd) plus the even harmonics plus a DC term, half the amplitude of the full-wave's " ++
        "pure-even doubling. Non-negatives pass unchanged; negatives fold to silence (−1→0, −100→0, −12345→0, sample_min→0); there is no rail edge; " ++
        "every output is NON-NEGATIVE and the map is IDEMPOTENT (a non-negative sample maps to itself); the half-wave output is the full-wave output " ++
        "MASKED to the positive half (+a,−a → a,0 where full-wave gave a,a); the span discipline holds; a span past the end refuses BadRange, the clip " ++
        "untouched. The map is MEMORYLESS; one compare-and-keep per sample; no gain, no ceiling, no threshold, no saturate, no delay line, no " ++
        "anti-aliasing, no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate. The half-wave rectifier completes the RECTIFIER " ++
        "PAIR beside the DRIVE clippers; an inverted half-wave (keep the negative half, zero the positive) is its plain sibling\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
